  "response": "The physiological case for cohesion-tension was built against a long tradition of attributing sap ascent to forces generated at the root. Henry Dixon and John Joly proposed the cohesion-tension theory of sap ascent in the mid-1890s, a proposal that came more than a century and a half before Stephen Hales measured root pressure in grapevines in his 1727 work Vegetable Staticks, a quantitative demonstration. Their date of formal publication has a fixed place in the historical record: that is, Dixon and Joly first published the cohesion-tension theory in 1924. From its earliest framing, the theory held that evaporation from leaves, rather than any push from below, supplies the energy for upward flow, and that the continuity of the water column in the conduits transmits this pull to the roots.\n\nThe quantitative difficulty facing the older view is easily stated. Measured root pressures in most plants rarely exceed about 0.5 megapascals, whereas lifting water to the top of a 100-meter tree requires a pressure gradient of roughly 1 megapascal to overcome gravity alone, before any allowance for frictional resistance in the xylem. Set against those modest figures for most plants, root pressure in conifers routinely exceeds 3 megapascals during summer transpiration. The physiologist therefore confronts two candidate mechanisms, and at least one of them must operate, perhaps both: water rising to the crown of a tree is pulled upward by tension in the xylem, or it is pushed upward by root pressure. Where that push operates, it is the cause of the elevated conifer pressures, since the driving force exerted from below is what raises root pressure in those species to such values during the transpiring season.\n\nThe decisive question, and the one that sustained the dispute for decades, was whether a column of water could be placed under substantial negative pressure without cavitating. Here physical chemistry supplied an answer. The cohesive strength of water allows xylem sap to sustain tensions of several megapascals. In other words, xylem sap can be held under several megapascals of negative pressure without its water column breaking. That cohesive strength confirms the most striking laboratory result in the literature: Lyman Briggs measured water sustaining tensions of about 26 megapascals in a spinning Z-shaped glass tube in 1950. The centrifugal method, by generating tension at the axis of rotation in a sealed and degassed sample, removed the objection that water in a vessel must inevitably separate from its walls or nucleate bubbles at modest tensions, and it placed the theoretical ceiling far above anything a tree would require.\n\nField validation proved harder, because measuring tension inside a functioning conduit risks disturbing the very quantity being measured. Although Ulrich Zimmermann and colleagues used a cell pressure probe in the 1990s to challenge the cohesion-tension theory, xylem pressure probe readings in transpiring plants agreed with pressure chamber readings of xylem tension. The interpretive situation admitted no middle ground: either that agreement held, or xylem pressure probe readings in transpiring plants disagreed with pressure chamber readings of xylem tension, and the two cannot both be true, while one of them must hold. Zimmermann's group had reported probe values far less negative than those inferred from the Scholander pressure chamber, and had argued that the chamber systematically overstated tension. The tension between that challenge and the concordant probe data was settled when Holbrook and colleagues concluded that pressure chamber measurements accurately reflect the tension in xylem sap, a finding obtained by subjecting stems to known negative pressures in a centrifuge and confirming that the chamber recovered them.\n\nThe resulting account integrates history, physics, and measurement. Root pressure remains a real phenomenon, but the weight of evidence attributes sustained ascent in tall trees to a metastable column held together by cohesion and drawn upward by transpirational tension, and the pressure chamber has been vindicated as a reliable instrument for quantifying that tension in the field.",
